# Line endings, for the files where they are not cosmetic.
#
# Nothing here is fixing a problem that exists today: every shell script in this
# repository is stored with LF, and a Linux clone runs them. This file is a
# guard, and it appeared with `deploy/` because that is the first tree written
# to be cloned onto a Linux server by someone other than its author.
#
# What it guards against is cheap and specific. This checkout is developed on
# Windows with core.autocrlf=true, and a bash script that reaches a Linux box
# with CRLF does not run at all: the kernel reads the shebang as
# `/usr/bin/env bash\r` and answers "bad interpreter". One editor or generator
# that writes CRLF, one commit, and the failure lands on the server rather than
# on the machine that caused it.
#
# The .cmd files are the other way round — cmd.exe wants CRLF, and they have it.

*.sh text eol=lf
deploy/dsh-user text eol=lf
deploy/Caddyfile text eol=lf
deploy/dsh@.service text eol=lf
deploy/users.conf.example text eol=lf

bin/ainar text eol=lf
bin/deck text eol=lf
bin/deck-build text eol=lf
bin/sample text eol=lf

*.cmd text eol=crlf
